<section>
  <h2>About the book</h2>
  <p>It began as a whisper. A strange silence in one ear, a fog he could not name, and then five words that changed everything: you have a brain tumour.</p>

  <p>Amir Khesro had built his life on certainty. The first in his family to leave a small farming village for university, he became a scientist, a teacher, a husband and a father of daughters. Then an acoustic neuroma, five centimetres wide and pressing on his brainstem, dismantled that certainty in a single hospital appointment.</p>

  <p>Whispers Through the Fog is the story of what happened next. The surgery and the long, humbling recovery. The wife who held a family together while her husband learned to walk again. The daughters who became his reason to keep going. The friends in Stoke-on-Trent whose quiet kindness carried him when he could not carry himself. And the fog itself, the invisible part of illness that no scan can show and no visitor can see.</p>

  <p>This is not a story tied up in neat answers. It is an honest, tender and often surprising account of losing the life you planned and slowly building the one that remains, told with a scientist’s clarity and a storyteller’s heart.</p>

  <p>If you are facing your own diagnosis, caring for someone who is, or simply searching for proof that broken things can mend, these pages were written for you.</p>

  <p>This revised and expanded second edition includes new chapters and new reflections written two years on from diagnosis.</p>
</section>
</div>

